The '''Mercedes-Benz SL-Class''' (marketed as '''[[Mercedes-AMG SL-Class (R232)|Mercedes-AMG SL]]''' since 2022) is a [[grand touring]] [[sports car]] manufactured by [[Mercedes-Benz]] since 1954. The designation "''SL"'' derives from the [[German language|German]] term "''Super-Leicht",'' which translates to "Super Light" in English.

Initially, the first [[Mercedes-Benz W194|300 SL]] was a [[Sports car racing|racing sports car]] built in 1952 
with no intention of developing a street version. In 1954, an [[United States|American]] importer [[Max Hoffman]] suggested the street version of 300 SL for the wealthy performance car enthusiasts in the [[United States]] where the market for the [[personal luxury car]] was booming after the [[Second World War]].
{{TOC_left}}
{{clear}}

==Abbreviation of SL==
Whether the abbreviation ''SL'' was derived from a 1931 [[Mercedes-Benz S-Series#Model SSKL (WS06RS)|Mercedes-Benz SSKL]] (Super Sport Kurz Leicht—Super Sport Short Light) isn't known. At the public introduction of [[Mercedes-Benz W194|300 SL]] in 1952, Mercedes-Benz did not define the abbreviation ''SL''.

The German magazine, {{lang|de|[[Auto Motor und Sport]]}}, declared in its 2012 special edition issue of Mercedes-Benz SL that [[Rudolf Uhlenhaut]]—in his notarised letter—indicated the abbreviation meant ''Super Leicht''.<ref name="AMS-Heft10-2012">{{citation|surname1=Jochen Übler |periodical=Auto, Motor und Sport, Mercedes SL-Spezial 2012 |title=Trimm's Leicht |issue=10| at=pp.&nbsp;14–17| issn=0005-0806| date=19 April 2012| language=de}}</ref> This contradicted with the abbreviation proposed by Engelen, Riedner, and Seufert who worked with Rudolf Uhlenhaut: they indicated the abbreviation means ''Sport Leicht''.<ref>{{citation|surname1=Günter Engelen|surname2=Mike Riedner|surname3=Hans-Dieter Seufert |title=Mercedes-Benz 300 SL |edition=2 |at=p.&nbsp;31 |isbn=3-613-01268-5 |date=1999 |publisher=Motorbuch-Verlag |language=de}}</ref> On 15 March 2021, {{lang|de|Auto Motor und Sport}} published a photocopy of an undated "Informations-Unterlage" (press release) from 1952 in its issue, showing the definition of ''SL'' as ''<u>s</u>uper-<u>l</u>eicht''.<ref>{{cite magazine|magazine=auto motor und sport |lang=de |date=15 March 2021 |department=Intro |title=Mercedes SL: Allrad, 800 PS, AMG-Technik |issue=8/2021 |page=13}}</ref>

Mercedes-Benz used ''Sport Leicht'' and ''Super Leicht'' interchangeably until 2017 when a chance discovery in its corporate archive clarified the abbreviation stood for "Super Leicht".<ref name="Classic-1-2002">{{citation|surname1=[[Gerold Lingnau]] |periodical=Mercedes-Benz Classic |title=300SL – Die Ikone wird fünfzig |issue=1 |at=pp.&nbsp;10–22 |issn=1610-8043 |date=2002 |language=de}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://media.daimler.com/marsMediaSite/ko/de/9904191 |title=Motorsport als Zündfunke: Der Mercedes-Benz 300 SL Rennsportwagen der Baureihe W 194 (1952–1953) |language=de |accessdate=25 July 2017 |work=Daimler Media}}</ref>

According to the [[German language|German]] definition, ''Leicht'' means ''light'' (as in weight, amount, and feeling, for instance) or ''easy'' (as in little effort). For this abbreviation in English language, the definition ''light'' is used.

